-- Q1: medium / L3 — Shares held in escrow subject to return: excluded until contingency resolved
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Dalton Corp. acquired a target company on January 1, 2025, issuing 100,000 shares to the former owners, of which 25,000 were placed in escrow pending resolution of a tax indemnity claim. If the indemnity is triggered, the escrowed shares are returned to Dalton and cancelled. As of December 31, 2025, the claim is still unresolved. Dalton has 400,000 other shares outstanding and net income of $850,000. How are the escrowed shares treated for basic EPS?', '["All 100,000 acquisition shares are included because they were legally issued", "Only the 75,000 non-escrowed shares are included; the 25,000 escrowed shares are contingently returnable and excluded from basic EPS", "The 25,000 escrowed shares are included in diluted EPS but not basic EPS", "None of the 100,000 acquisition shares are included until the escrow period expires"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-48, shares subject to contingent return are contingently issuable shares in reverse — they are currently outstanding but may be returned. The 25,000 escrowed shares are excluded from basic EPS because their continued outstanding status depends on the unresolved contingency. Basic EPS denominator = 400,000 + 75,000 = 475,000; EPS = $850,000/475,000 = $1.79. Choice A incorrectly treats all issued shares as permanently outstanding despite the return contingency. The escrowed shares may be included in diluted EPS if return becomes remote.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q2: medium / L2 — Forfeiture of unvested restricted shares without dividend rights
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Fernbrook Inc. had 200,000 total shares outstanding on January 1, 2025, of which 20,000 are unvested restricted shares without dividend rights. On July 1, employees forfeit 5,000 of the unvested shares. What is the effect of the forfeiture on the basic EPS denominator?', '["The denominator decreases by 2,500 shares for the half-year weighting", "The forfeiture has no effect on the basic EPS denominator", "The denominator increases by 2,500 shares because fewer shares will ultimately vest", "The denominator decreases by 5,000 shares for the full year"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-13, unvested restricted shares without dividend rights (or with forfeitable dividends) are excluded from the basic EPS denominator. Since these 20,000 shares were never in the basic EPS weighted-average, their forfeiture has no effect on the basic EPS denominator. The forfeiture does reduce the incremental shares included in diluted EPS via the treasury stock method, but it does not change basic EPS.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q3: easy / L1 — Full valuation allowance effect on if-converted numerator
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Under ASC 260, when an entity has a full valuation allowance against its deferred tax assets, what is the effect on the if-converted numerator adjustment for convertible bonds?', '["The after-tax interest is added back using the statutory rate regardless of the valuation allowance", "No interest adjustment is made to the numerator", "The full pretax interest expense is added back because no tax benefit exists from the deduction", "Only the portion of interest covered by the valuation allowance is added back"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-40, the if-converted method adds back interest net of actual tax effects. When a full valuation allowance exists, the entity derives no incremental tax benefit from the interest deduction, so removing the interest (assuming conversion) does not change taxes. The full pretax interest is therefore added back. Choice A incorrectly applies the statutory rate when the valuation allowance eliminates the benefit entirely.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q4: easy / L2 — Subsidiary holding parent shares treated as consolidated treasury stock
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Larkspur Corp. owns 80% of Sundale Inc. Sundale holds 10,000 shares of Larkspur common stock. Larkspur has 500,000 shares issued and outstanding on a standalone basis. For computing consolidated basic EPS, how should the 10,000 shares held by Sundale be treated?', '["Included in the weighted-average because Sundale is a separate legal entity", "Excluded only to the extent of Larkspur''s 80% ownership interest (8,000 shares)", "Included in the denominator with a disclosure of the intercompany holding", "Excluded from the weighted-average because they are treated as treasury shares of the consolidated entity"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-10 and consolidation principles, shares of a parent held by a consolidated subsidiary are treated as treasury stock from the consolidated group perspective. All 10,000 shares are excluded from the consolidated weighted-average, not just the parent''s 80% proportionate share. Choice A incorrectly ignores the elimination of intercompany holdings required in consolidation. The 10,000 shares are not outstanding from the consolidated entity''s viewpoint.', 'easy', 2),

-- Q5: hard / L4 — Back-calculate maximum dilutive options before EPS falls below target
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Stanton Corp. has net income to common of $2,000,000 and 500,000 weighted-average shares. The board wants diluted EPS to remain at or above $3.80. The average market price is $60, and any new option grants would carry a $40 exercise price. Under the treasury stock method, what is the maximum number of stock options that can be outstanding without diluted EPS falling below $3.80?', '["78,947", "52,631", "26,315", "105,263"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-22, each option adds incremental shares equal to options minus shares repurchased. Incremental per option = 1 - ($40/$60) = 1/3. Let x = total options. Constraint: $2,000,000 / (500,000 + x/3) >= $3.80. Solving: 500,000 + x/3 <= 526,315.8, so x/3 <= 26,315.8, and x <= 78,947. At 78,947 options, incremental shares = 26,316, giving diluted EPS of $2,000,000/526,316 = $3.80. Choice B (52,631) incorrectly uses the full option count as the constraint rather than the one-third incremental share effect.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q6: easy / L1 — EPS denominator excludes shares not yet issued under a stock appreciation right settled in shares
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Winfield Corp. granted stock appreciation rights (SARs) to executives that will be settled by issuing common shares equal in value to the appreciation. No SARs have been exercised as of December 31, 2025. Under ASC 260, are the potential SAR settlement shares included in the basic EPS denominator?', '["Yes, because the SARs will be settled in shares", "No, share-settled SARs are excluded from basic EPS but included in diluted EPS via the treasury stock method if dilutive", "Yes, but only if the SARs are vested as of the reporting date", "No, SARs are excluded from both basic and diluted EPS because they are compensation instruments"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-22, share-settled SARs are treated like stock options for diluted EPS purposes and included via the treasury stock method when dilutive. However, because no shares have been issued, they are excluded from basic EPS, which counts only shares actually outstanding. Choice A incorrectly includes unissued shares in the basic calculation. Choice D incorrectly excludes SARs from diluted EPS — their potential share issuance makes them potentially dilutive securities.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q7: medium / L3 — Large stock distribution (>25%) treated as stock split
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Havenbrook Inc. had 200,000 shares outstanding and declared a 50% stock distribution on September 1, distributing 100,000 additional shares. Net income for the year is $600,000 with no preferred stock. The controller weighted the new shares from September 1, computing weighted-average shares as 233,333. Evaluate whether the controller''s computation is correct.', '["The controller is correct; basic EPS is $2.57", "The controller is correct because distributions above 25% require weighted calculations", "The controller is incorrect; the distribution is treated like a stock split, and basic EPS is $2.00", "The controller is incorrect; only the first 25% is retroactive, and basic EPS is $2.18"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 505-20-25-3 and ASC 260-10-55-12, a stock distribution exceeding 20-25% of previously outstanding shares is accounted for similarly to a stock split. The 300,000 post-distribution shares are applied retroactively to January 1. Weighted-average = 300,000 for the full year; basic EPS = $600,000 / 300,000 = $2.00. The controller erred by weighting from September 1, which is appropriate only for small stock dividends (under 20-25%).', 'medium', 3),

-- Q8: hard / L4 — Induced conversion sweetener: effect on diluted EPS in period offered
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Ravenscroft Inc. has $1,000,000 of 5% convertible bonds originally convertible into 20,000 shares. To induce early conversion, Ravenscroft offers an additional 3,000 shares as a sweetener for conversions within 60 days. All bondholders convert during the offer period. The tax rate is 25%. In the period the inducement is offered, evaluate how the sweetener affects EPS.', '["The 3,000 additional shares are ignored for diluted EPS because they are a one-time inducement", "The inducement is treated as a dividend to converting holders and does not affect the EPS denominator", "The 3,000 sweetener shares are included as additional dilutive shares in the diluted EPS denominator, and the fair value of the sweetener is charged against retained earnings and deducted from the numerator", "Only the original 20,000 conversion shares affect diluted EPS; the sweetener is disclosed in the notes only"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 470-20-40-16 and ASC 260-10-45-40, when an issuer offers a sweetener to induce conversion, the fair value of the additional consideration (3,000 shares) is recognized as an expense (inducement charge) and is also deducted from the EPS numerator. The 3,000 additional shares plus the original 20,000 shares enter the diluted denominator. Choice A incorrectly ignores the sweetener''s dilutive impact. The inducement expense reduces the numerator while the extra shares increase the denominator, compounding the dilutive effect.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q9: medium / L2 — Multiple classes of common stock: separate EPS for each class
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Fairhaven Corp. has two classes of common stock: Class A (200,000 shares, $1.00 per share dividend right) and Class B (100,000 shares, $0.50 per share dividend right). Net income is $500,000 and all earnings are distributed as dividends. Under ASC 260-10-45-60, what disclosure is required?', '["A single EPS figure computed using total shares of both classes combined", "Separate EPS for each class of common stock presented on the face of the income statement", "EPS only for the class with the larger dividend right", "A weighted-average EPS blending both classes proportionally"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-60, when an entity has multiple classes of common stock, per-share amounts must be presented for each class using the two-class method. Class A receives $200,000 ($1.00 x 200,000) and Class B receives $50,000 ($0.50 x 100,000), totaling $250,000 in dividends. The remaining $250,000 undistributed earnings are allocated based on participation rights. Each class gets its own EPS line on the income statement. Choice A incorrectly merges distinct classes with different economic rights.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q10: hard / L4 — Subsidiary with its own dilutive securities: effect on consolidated diluted EPS
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Halcyon Corp. owns 70% of Verdant Inc. Verdant has net income of $500,000, 100,000 common shares, and 10,000 in-the-money stock options producing 3,000 incremental shares via the treasury stock method. No intercompany transactions exist. Evaluate how Verdant''s dilutive options affect Halcyon''s consolidated diluted EPS.', '["Verdant''s options are ignored because they do not dilute the parent''s shares", "Verdant''s options dilute the parent''s ownership percentage in Verdant, reducing the earnings attributable to the parent and therefore reducing consolidated diluted EPS", "Verdant''s options are included by adding 3,000 shares to the consolidated denominator", "Verdant''s options increase consolidated EPS because the noncontrolling interest absorbs the dilution"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-11 and 260-10-55-64, when a subsidiary has its own dilutive securities, those securities dilute the parent''s ownership interest in the subsidiary''s earnings. Verdant''s diluted EPS denominator increases from 100,000 to 103,000. The parent''s share of Verdant''s earnings drops from 70% of $500,000 = $350,000 to 70% x ($500,000/103,000) x 100,000 = $339,806 (approximately). This reduction flows into the consolidated numerator. Choice C incorrectly adds subsidiary-level incremental shares to the parent''s denominator rather than adjusting the numerator.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q11: medium / L3 — Weighted-average for entity with short fiscal period
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Ridgepoint Inc. was incorporated on April 1, 2025, issuing 120,000 shares on that date. The company adopted a fiscal year ending December 31. No other share transactions occurred. Net income from April 1 through December 31 is $270,000 with no preferred stock. What weighted-average share count should Ridgepoint use for basic EPS for its initial nine-month fiscal period?', '["30,000", "60,000", "120,000", "90,000"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-10, the weighted-average is computed over the entity''s reporting period. For Ridgepoint''s initial nine-month fiscal period (April 1 through December 31), the 120,000 shares were outstanding for the entire reporting period. The weighted-average is 120,000 and basic EPS = $270,000 / 120,000 = $2.25. Choice D (90,000) incorrectly weights the shares as 9/12 of a hypothetical full calendar year rather than using the actual reporting period.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q12: medium / L2 — Stock subscriptions: shares not yet issued excluded from basic EPS
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'On January 1, 2025, Cedarbrook Corp. had 200,000 shares outstanding and entered into binding stock subscription agreements for 20,000 additional shares at $45 per share. The subscribers made partial payments throughout 2025, but no shares were issued because the full subscription price had not been received by December 31. Are the 20,000 subscribed shares included in the 2025 basic EPS denominator?', '["Yes, included from January 1 because the agreements are legally binding", "Yes, but weighted proportionally to the percentage of subscription price received", "No, because shares under subscription are not outstanding until actually issued", "Yes, included as contingently issuable shares at year-end"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-10, the basic EPS denominator includes only shares actually outstanding. Stock subscriptions represent binding contracts to purchase shares, but the shares are not issued or outstanding until the full subscription price is received and the shares are formally issued. Until then, they do not participate in earnings or voting. Choice A incorrectly conflates a binding agreement with actual share issuance.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q13: easy / L1 — Per-share amounts required for continuing operations and net income
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Under ASC 260-10-45-2, a public entity with a complex capital structure must present per-share amounts for which of the following income statement line items?', '["Net income only", "Gross profit and net income", "Operating income and net income", "Income from continuing operations and net income"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-2, entities are required to present per-share amounts for income from continuing operations and for net income on the face of the income statement. Per-share amounts for discontinued operations may be presented either on the face of the income statement or in the notes. Gross profit and operating income do not require per-share presentation under ASC 260.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q14: medium / L3 — Contingently issuable shares tied to a share price milestone
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Wexford Corp. acquired a subsidiary and agreed to issue 30,000 additional shares to the former owners if Wexford''s stock price reaches $80 by December 31, 2026. At June 30, 2025 (interim reporting date), the stock price is $85 and has been above $80 for three consecutive months. For the six-month interim period ending June 30, 2025, should the 30,000 shares be included in diluted EPS?', '["No, because the contingency period does not end until December 31, 2026", "Yes, because the share price condition is currently satisfied as of the interim reporting date", "No, because market-price contingencies are never included until the contingency is resolved", "Yes, but only 15,000 shares (half-year weighting) should be included"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-48, contingently issuable shares are included in diluted EPS if all necessary conditions are currently met, assuming the end of the reporting period were the end of the contingency period. At June 30, 2025, the $80 price target is met ($85 current price), so the 30,000 shares are included in diluted EPS for the interim period. Choice A incorrectly defers inclusion until the contingency formally expires. No time-weighting is needed because the condition is binary—either met or not.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q15: easy / L1 — Retroactive restatement of prior-period EPS for stock dividend
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Under ASC 260-10-55-12, when a company distributes a 10% stock dividend in the current year, how are prior-period earnings per share amounts reported in comparative financial statements?', '["Prior-period EPS is not restated; only a footnote disclosure is required", "Prior-period EPS is restated only if the stock dividend exceeds 20%", "Prior-period EPS is restated only for the most recent prior year presented", "Prior-period EPS is retroactively adjusted to reflect the stock dividend as though it occurred at the beginning of the earliest period presented"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 260-10-55-12, stock dividends and stock splits are given retroactive treatment for EPS purposes. All prior-period EPS figures presented in comparative statements are restated as though the stock dividend occurred at the beginning of the earliest period. This applies regardless of the size of the stock dividend. Choice A is incorrect because retroactive adjustment (not mere disclosure) is required. Choice B incorrectly imposes a size threshold.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q16: easy / L1 — Shares issued in a stock dividend are not weighted from the distribution date
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Thornbury Corp. had 400,000 shares outstanding and distributed a 5% stock dividend on October 1, 2025, issuing 20,000 additional shares. A newly hired accountant weighted the 20,000 shares from October 1, computing a weighted-average of 405,000. Is the accountant correct?', '["Yes, stock dividend shares are weighted from the distribution date like any new issuance", "Yes, but the weighted-average should be 410,000 because the shares are weighted for four months", "No, the 20,000 shares are applied retroactively to January 1, making the weighted-average 420,000", "No, the 20,000 shares are excluded from the denominator entirely until the following year"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-55-12, shares issued in a stock dividend are given retroactive treatment and applied to the beginning of the earliest period presented, unlike shares issued for cash which are weighted from the issuance date. The weighted-average is 420,000 (400,000 + 20,000) for the full year. The accountant erred by weighting the stock dividend shares from October 1 as though they were a new issuance for consideration. Retroactive treatment ensures comparability across periods.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q17: medium / L2 — Rights issue with below-market element: bonus factor applied retroactively
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Kensington Corp. had 300,000 shares outstanding and issued rights to all shareholders to purchase one new share for every three held at $20 (market price on exercise date: $32). All rights were exercised on October 1, 2025. Under ASC 260-10-55-22, which statement about the weighted-average computation is correct?', '["The 100,000 new shares are simply weighted from October 1 with no adjustment to prior shares", "A bonus element factor is applied to shares outstanding before the rights exercise, and the new shares are weighted from October 1", "The rights issue is treated identically to a stock split with no weighting from the exercise date", "Only the excess of market price over the subscription price is weighted from October 1"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-55-22, a rights issue at below market price contains a bonus element. The theoretical ex-rights price is calculated: [(300,000 x $32) + (100,000 x $20)] / 400,000 = $29. A bonus factor of $32/$29 = 1.103 is applied retroactively to shares outstanding before the exercise date, and the 100,000 new shares are weighted from October 1. Choice A ignores the below-market bonus element. Choice C incorrectly treats it as a pure stock split.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q18: easy / L1 — Nonpublic entity EPS presentation: voluntary vs. required
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'A nonpublic entity that does not have publicly traded debt or equity chooses not to present EPS on its income statement. Under ASC 260-10-15-2, is this acceptable?', '["No, all entities preparing GAAP financial statements must present EPS", "No, nonpublic entities must present basic EPS but may omit diluted EPS", "Yes, ASC 260 does not require nonpublic entities to present EPS, though they may voluntarily do so", "Yes, but only if the entity has fewer than 100 shareholders"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-15-2, EPS presentation is required only for entities with publicly traded common stock or potential common stock, or entities that have filed or are in the process of filing with a regulatory agency for public sale. Nonpublic entities are not required to present EPS but may elect to do so voluntarily, in which case they must follow ASC 260 in full. Choice A incorrectly extends the scope to all GAAP reporters.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q19: medium / L2 — Spin-off distribution: EPS restatement for prior periods
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'On September 1, 2025, Oakdale Corp. completed a spin-off of its subsidiary by distributing one share of the subsidiary for every four shares of Oakdale common stock. The distribution reduced Oakdale''s share price proportionally. Under ASC 260-10-55-12A, how should Oakdale present EPS for comparative prior periods?', '["Prior-period EPS is not restated because a spin-off is not a stock split or dividend", "Prior-period EPS is retroactively adjusted using a price-adjustment factor to reflect the reduction in per-share value from the spin-off", "Prior-period EPS is restated by increasing the prior denominator by 25% for the subsidiary shares distributed", "Prior-period EPS is restated only if the spin-off is tax-free under IRC Section 355"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-55-12A, a spin-off distribution that reduces the parent''s share price is analogous to a stock dividend and requires retroactive adjustment of prior-period EPS. The adjustment uses a price-ratio factor (pre-distribution price / ex-distribution price) applied to historical share counts. This ensures comparability across periods by reflecting the economic dilution from the distribution. Choice C incorrectly adds subsidiary shares to the parent''s denominator; the subsidiary has its own separate EPS.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q20: medium / L3 — Contingently issuable shares: earnings target met at interim date
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Ashworth Corp. agreed to issue 50,000 additional shares to selling shareholders if 2025 annual net income exceeds $5 million. Through June 30, year-to-date net income is $6 million. The controller excludes the 50,000 shares from the June 30 interim diluted EPS computation because the annual target has not been finalized. Evaluate the controller''s treatment.', '["The controller is correct because annual earnings targets can only be evaluated at year-end", "The controller is correct because contingently issuable shares affect only basic EPS, not diluted", "The controller is incorrect; the shares should be included in basic EPS rather than diluted EPS", "The controller is incorrect; under ASC 260-10-45-48, the shares are included in diluted EPS because the earnings condition is currently met as of the interim reporting date"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-48, contingently issuable shares are included in diluted EPS if all necessary conditions would be satisfied assuming the end of the reporting period were the end of the contingency period. Since year-to-date income of $6 million exceeds the $5 million target as of June 30, the contingency is currently met and the 50,000 shares are included in the interim diluted EPS denominator. The controller erred by deferring the evaluation until year-end.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q21: medium / L2 — ESOP shares: allocated vs. unallocated treatment in basic EPS
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Dunbarton Corp. sponsors a leveraged ESOP that holds 50,000 shares of Dunbarton common stock, of which 30,000 are allocated to participant accounts and 20,000 remain unallocated (held as collateral for the ESOP loan). Dunbarton has 400,000 shares issued. For basic EPS, which shares are included in the weighted-average denominator?', '["All 50,000 ESOP shares because the ESOP is a consolidated entity", "Only the 30,000 allocated shares; unallocated shares are excluded", "Neither allocated nor unallocated shares because all ESOP shares are treated as treasury stock", "Only the 20,000 unallocated shares because they have not been earned by employees"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 718-40-45-3 (formerly SOP 93-6), only allocated ESOP shares are considered outstanding for basic EPS purposes. The 30,000 allocated shares are included in the weighted-average denominator. The 20,000 unallocated shares are excluded because they have not been released to participants and are analogous to unissued shares. Choice A incorrectly includes unreleased collateral shares. The distinction between allocated and unallocated is critical for ESOP EPS treatment.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q22: medium / L3 — Semi-annual coupon: full-year interest for if-converted
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Haverfield Corp. issued $1,000,000 of 6% convertible bonds on January 1, 2025, paying interest semi-annually on June 30 and December 31. The bonds were outstanding all year and are convertible into 25,000 shares. The tax rate is 25%. For diluted EPS, the controller adds back only $22,500 (one semi-annual payment of $30,000 after tax). Evaluate whether this approach is correct.', '["The controller is correct; only one coupon payment applies", "The controller is incorrect; the add-back should be $45,000 (full-year interest of $60,000 after tax)", "The controller is incorrect; the add-back should be $60,000 (full pretax annual interest)", "The controller is correct because semi-annual bonds require a half-year adjustment"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-40, the if-converted method assumes conversion at the beginning of the period. When bonds are outstanding for the full year, the numerator adjustment includes the full year''s interest expense regardless of payment frequency. Annual interest = $60,000; after-tax = $60,000 x 0.75 = $45,000. The controller erred by using only one semi-annual payment. The interest payment schedule does not limit the if-converted adjustment.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q23: hard / L4 — Recapitalization: exchange of old shares for new shares with different rights
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'On April 1, 2025, Heathrow Corp. completes a recapitalization in which each existing common share is exchanged for 0.8 shares of new common stock plus 0.2 shares of new nonparticipating preferred stock. Before the exchange, 500,000 common shares were outstanding. Evaluate how the recapitalization affects the 2025 basic EPS computation.', '["Use 500,000 shares for the full year because the total number of securities received equals the shares surrendered", "Use 400,000 new common shares retroactively adjusted to January 1 for the full year", "Use a weighted-average reflecting 500,000 old shares for Q1 and 400,000 new shares for Q2-Q4", "Use 400,000 shares from April 1 forward and 500,000 shares for Q1, weighted proportionally"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-55-12, a recapitalization that changes the number of common shares without consideration (akin to a reverse split) is treated retroactively. The exchange yields 400,000 new common shares (500,000 x 0.8). This is applied to January 1 of the earliest period presented because the exchange is a restructuring of capital, not a new issuance for value. The 100,000 nonparticipating preferred shares are excluded from the common EPS denominator. Choice C incorrectly weights the exchange from April 1 as though new shares were issued for cash.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q24: hard / L4 — Consolidated EPS with noncontrolling interest and parent-level options
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Halford Group consolidates an 80%-owned subsidiary. Consolidated net income is $1,000,000, of which $120,000 is attributable to the noncontrolling interest. Halford has 200,000 parent-company shares and stock options producing 10,000 incremental shares via the treasury stock method. The subsidiary has no potentially dilutive securities of its own. Evaluate consolidated diluted EPS.', '["($1,000,000 - $120,000) / (200,000 + 10,000) = $4.19", "($1,000,000 - $120,000) / 200,000 = $4.40 because subsidiary options, not parent options, drive consolidation dilution", "$1,000,000 / (200,000 + 10,000) = $4.76 because noncontrolling interest is not deducted in consolidated EPS", "($1,000,000 - $120,000) / 210,000 = $4.19, but the subsidiary''s dilutive securities must also be included even if none exist"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-11 and consolidation guidance, consolidated EPS uses income attributable to the parent ($880,000) as the numerator. The noncontrolling interest of $120,000 is deducted because it does not belong to common shareholders. The denominator includes the parent''s 200,000 shares plus the 10,000 incremental shares from parent-level options. Diluted EPS = $880,000/210,000 = $4.19. Choice C incorrectly omits the NCI deduction, which would overstate EPS attributable to parent shareholders.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q25: medium / L3 — Share cancellation vs treasury: same EPS effect
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Meridian Systems had 400,000 common shares outstanding on January 1, 2025. On July 1, the company repurchased 40,000 shares. Company A retires the shares permanently; Company B holds identical shares as treasury stock. Net income is $1,080,000 for both. Compare the basic EPS of the two companies.', '["Both companies report the same basic EPS of $2.84", "Company A has higher EPS because retired shares are removed from the denominator permanently", "Company B has higher EPS because treasury shares can be reissued", "Company A reports $2.70 and Company B reports $3.00"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-10, the weighted-average depends on shares outstanding, not on whether repurchased shares are retired or held as treasury stock. In both cases, 40,000 shares leave the outstanding count on July 1. Weighted-average = (400,000 x 6/12) + (360,000 x 6/12) = 380,000. Basic EPS = $1,080,000/380,000 = $2.84 for both. The legal treatment (retirement vs. treasury) does not affect EPS computation.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q26: easy / L1 — Period-end shares vs weighted-average: rationale
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Under ASC 260, why is the weighted-average number of common shares used rather than the period-end share count as the denominator in basic EPS?', '["To reduce the denominator and increase reported EPS", "To reflect the time period over which shares participated in generating earnings", "To match the share count with the balance sheet date", "To eliminate the effect of stock dividends and splits"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-10, the weighted-average captures the time-based relationship between shares outstanding and the earnings they helped generate. Shares issued mid-year contributed to earnings only from their issuance date, so weighting them for the partial period provides a more meaningful per-share measure. Choice C is incorrect because using the period-end count would mismatch the denominator with earnings by including shares not outstanding for the entire period.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q27: medium / L2 — Treasury share donation increases outstanding shares from donation date
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Caldwell Enterprises had 300,000 common shares outstanding on January 1, 2025. On that date, Caldwell also held 12,000 treasury shares. On May 1, the company donated all 12,000 treasury shares to a charitable foundation. What is the weighted-average number of common shares for basic EPS?', '["300,000", "308,000", "306,000", "312,000"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-10, the denominator uses shares outstanding, weighted for the portion of the period they were outstanding. Treasury shares are not outstanding, but when Caldwell donates them to a foundation, the shares are effectively reissued — the foundation holds them as outstanding shares from May 1. Weighted-average = (300,000 x 4/12) + (312,000 x 8/12) = 100,000 + 208,000 = 308,000. Choice A (300,000) incorrectly assumes donated treasury shares remain excluded from the outstanding count. Choice D (312,000) incorrectly treats the shares as outstanding for the full year without weighting from the donation date.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q28: medium / L2 — Partially paid shares: proportional inclusion in basic EPS
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Chelmsford Corp. issued 40,000 shares on installment contracts. As of December 31, 2025, subscribers have paid 60% of the total subscription price. The shares carry dividend rights only to the extent paid. Under ASC 260-10-45-13, how should these partially paid shares be treated in the basic EPS denominator?', '["Excluded entirely until the full subscription price is received", "Included at 24,000 shares (40,000 x 60%) based on the paid-in fraction and corresponding dividend rights", "Included at the full 40,000 shares because they have been legally issued", "Included at 16,000 shares representing the unpaid portion only"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-13, partially paid shares with dividend rights proportional to the amount paid are included in the basic EPS denominator based on the fraction paid. Since 60% of the subscription price has been received and dividends accrue on that basis, 24,000 share equivalents (40,000 x 60%) are included. Choice C incorrectly includes the full count regardless of participation rights. The proportional inclusion ensures the denominator reflects the shares'' actual claim on earnings.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q29: hard / L3 — Reverse treasury stock method for written call options
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Pemberton Corp. wrote call options allowing holders to purchase 50,000 shares at $30. The average market price is $45. Net income is $3,000,000 with 800,000 weighted-average shares. Using the reverse treasury stock method under ASC 260-10-45-35, calculate the incremental dilutive shares and the resulting diluted EPS.', '["16,667 incremental shares; diluted EPS = $3.67", "50,000 incremental shares; diluted EPS = $3.53", "33,333 incremental shares; diluted EPS = $3.60", "No incremental shares because written calls are always antidilutive"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-22 and 260-10-55-36, in-the-money written call options use the treasury stock method. Assumed proceeds = 50,000 x $30 = $1,500,000. Shares repurchased at market = $1,500,000/$45 = 33,333. Incremental shares = 50,000 - 33,333 = 16,667. Diluted EPS = $3,000,000/(800,000 + 16,667) = $3.67. Choice C (33,333) confuses repurchased shares with incremental shares. Written calls that are in the money are dilutive because more shares are issued than repurchased.', 'hard', 3),

-- Q30: easy / L1 — Cumulative preferred dividend deducted even in a net loss year
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Under ASC 260-10-45-11, when a public entity reports a net loss and has cumulative preferred stock outstanding with an annual dividend entitlement of $80,000 but no dividends are declared, how does the preferred stock affect the basic EPS numerator?', '["No deduction is made because dividends cannot be distributed from a net loss", "The $80,000 annual entitlement is deducted from the net loss, increasing the loss available to common shareholders", "The $80,000 is added to the net loss to partially offset the loss", "Only half the annual entitlement is deducted when the entity reports a loss"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-11, cumulative preferred dividends are deducted from net income (or added to net loss) in the EPS numerator regardless of whether dividends are declared and regardless of whether the entity is profitable. The $80,000 is deducted, making the loss to common shareholders larger. Choice A is incorrect because the EPS numerator adjustment is independent of the entity''s ability to pay dividends. The deduction ensures the loss reflects the common shareholders'' residual position.', 'easy', 1)
;

COMMIT;
